<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <!--
    Per-monitor DPI awareness, version 2, declared before anything can query it.

    # Why the version matters, and why only one setting is named

    `dpiAware` is the Windows 8.1 spelling and its `true/pm` value means
    per-monitor *version 1*. `dpiAwareness` is the Windows 10 spelling and is
    documented to take precedence where both appear. On this machine it did not:
    a manifest carrying both produced a window reporting
    DPI_AWARENESS_CONTEXT_PER_MONITOR_AWARE — v1 — read straight off the window
    with `GetWindowDpiAwarenessContext`.

    The difference is not academic. Under v1 the application must rescale its
    own non-client area, and Windows will not do it: the title bar keeps its old
    metrics, which is visible as a caption that is the wrong height and has lost
    its icon. Under v2 Windows scales the frame, the menus and the scroll bars
    itself. So the legacy line is gone rather than kept "for older Windows" —
    the supportedOS declaration below already says which Windows this is for.

    # Why a manifest rather than a call

    winit calls `SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext` when it creates the event loop,
    and for the application window that is enough. The agent has no event loop
    at all — it is a headless process on another desktop — and it converts
    normalised touch coordinates into physical pixels with `GetSystemMetrics`,
    which answers in scaled coordinates to a process that has not declared
    itself. The manifest is applied by the loader, so both binaries get the same
    answer with no ordering to arrange.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- PerMonitorV2 is honoured only for an application that says it was
           built for Windows 10 or later. -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
